/*
    `partial` marks its operand as potentially undefined without changing which values
    it admits or which results it produces.
    The compiler does not look through the marker to infer a narrower definedness
    condition; it assumes conservatively that the expression may be undefined.
    `partial` neither catches undefinedness nor turns it into a value.
    Contrast with domain restriction (`?`), which narrows the admitted domain instead.
*/

int n => partial (100 / n)
